window.docContents[23].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-3-___-Space-and-Time--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-3":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"3-3\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the coherent SI unit symbol. The citation travels with\nthe kind as data, so the source of each definition can be rendered or audited\ndownstream rather than kept in a comment.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. ISO 80000-3, Second edition, 2019-10 item 3-3. The catalogue lists all 42\nitems in item order — length (3-1.1), area (3-3), volume (3-4), duration (3-9), speed\n(3-10.2), frequency (3-17.1), … — each paired with its coherent SI unit symbol.\n\nArea is L² (item 3-3). The catalogued area kind carries length-exponent two in\nPhysLib's dimension group — the standard's dimensional statement reproduced as a checked\ncomputation, not an annotation. Uses the dimension map.\n\nProved as area_dim_length : area.dim.length = 2 in the Iso80000 library, discharged\nby Dim.area_length from the Dimension layer. The companion volume_dim_length\n(item 3-4) gives length-exponent three.\n\nSpeed is L·T⁻¹ (item 3-10.2). The catalogued speed kind's dimension equals length\ndivided by time — a checked computation in the dimension group. Uses\nthe dimension map.\n\nProved as speed_dim : speed.dim = Dim.length / Dim.time, discharged by Dim.speed_eq.\nThe catalogue also records curvature_dim_length = -1 (item 3-2, L⁻¹),\nfrequency_dim_time = -1 (item 3-17.1, T⁻¹), and acceleration_dim_time = -2\n(item 3-11, L·T⁻²).\n\nThe metre is a well-formed unit (item 3-1.1). Length is a ratio-scale kind, so it\nbears a unit (Dybkær §13.3.5); the metre is one such\nchosen reference.\n\nProved as metre_wellFormed : metre.WellFormed via\nKindOfProperty.rational_bears_unit.\n\nA metre and a second are not commensurable. Length and duration are distinct kinds,\nso their units cannot be compared by ratio — a type-level fact, not a runtime check.\nUses metrological units.\n\nProved as metre_second_not_commensurable : ¬ metre.Commensurable second by unfolding\nCommensurable to the underlying kinds and decide (length's kind id differs from\nduration's).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-3 — Space and Time","header":"19.1. Quantity-kinds and units of ISO 80000-3","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-3-___-Space-and-Time--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-3"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--CPU___-single-threaded-___-the-universal-baseline-and-the-oracle":{"contents":"Instantiate the model at the scalar executable carrier (α := Float). The kind overlay erases by\n.magnitude to the bare floating-point program of Step 4's payoff; every intermediate lives in a\nregister, so the arithmetic intensity per work-item is maximal and the kernel is compute-bound on one\ncore. This strategy is unconditional: it runs any science model, including those whose interleaving\nof I/O and computation, data-dependent iteration, or irregular memory access makes efficient\nparallelism genuinely hard — the cases where the later strategies do not apply. It is also the\noracle: because it is the same erased kernel, its per-pixel result is the reference the parallel and\nGPU strategies are checked against, bit for bit. The cost is that it uses a single core.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009Go fast, automatically — the deployment spectrum","header":"15.3.1. CPU, single-threaded — the universal baseline and the oracle","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--CPU___-single-threaded-___-the-universal-baseline-and-the-oracle"}});